<article class="env-definition" id="04E6">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04E6">Definition <span data-tag="04E6">4.17.1</span>.</a> Let $H : \mathcal{I} \to \mathcal{J}$ be a functor between categories. We say <em>$\mathcal{I}$ is cofinal in $\mathcal{J}$</em> or that $H$ is <em>cofinal</em> if </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
for all $y \in \mathop{\mathrm{Ob}}\nolimits (\mathcal{J})$ there exist an $x \in \mathop{\mathrm{Ob}}\nolimits (\mathcal{I})$ and a morphism $y \to H(x)$, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
given $y \in \mathop{\mathrm{Ob}}\nolimits (\mathcal{J})$, $x, x' \in \mathop{\mathrm{Ob}}\nolimits (\mathcal{I})$ and morphisms $y \to H(x)$ and $y \to H(x')$ there exist a sequence of morphisms 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  x = x_0 \leftarrow x_1 \rightarrow x_2 \leftarrow x_3 \rightarrow \ldots \rightarrow x_{2n} = x'  \]
</div>
<p>
 in $\mathcal{I}$ and morphisms $y \to H(x_ i)$ in $\mathcal{J}$ with $y \to H(x_0)$ and $y \to H(x_{2n})$ the given morphisms such that the diagrams 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ &  y \ar[ld] \ar[d] \ar[rd] \\  H(x_{2k}) &  H(x_{2k + 1}) \ar[l] \ar[r] &  H(x_{2k + 2}) }  \]
</div>
<p>
 commute for $k = 0, \ldots , n - 1$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> In other words, fixing an object $y$ of $\mathcal{J}$ consider the set $S$ of pairs $(x, b)$ where $x$ is an object of $\mathcal{I}$ and $b : y \to H(x)$ is a morphism. Consider the equivalence relation on $S$ generated by $(x, b) \sim (x', b')$ if there exists a morphism $a : x \to x'$ with $b' = H(a) \circ b$. Then $S$ should consist of exactly one equivalence class. </p>
</article>